[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 0:16] A COGS-first read. COGS is 58% of revenue today, up from 42% two years ago. What does that tell you, and what would you look at next?

[candidate · 0:22] That's essentially the whole story. COGS went from 42% to 58% of revenue, a 16-point increase, and total margin fell 18 points — so roughly 16 of those 18 points sit in COGS. The other two points are somewhere else, labor or overhead, but they're secondary.

What it tells me is that the cost of goods sold per dollar of revenue has risen sharply. That can happen three ways: input prices went up, the quantity of input per unit sold went up — waste, spoilage, bigger portions — or the mix shifted toward items that are inherently lower margin, like food or milk-heavy drinks versus plain brewed coffee. It could also be that prices to the customer stayed flat while input costs rose, which is really the same squeeze seen from the revenue side.

