[candidate · 0:32] Let me test it with numbers. Two years ago COGS was 42% of revenue. Beans at 25% of that is 10.5 points of revenue; other inputs are the remaining 75%, so 31.5 points.

If other inputs rose 37.5% and nothing else changed, that 31.5 becomes 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3 points. Add beans at 10.5 and you get 53.8 points. But actual COGS is 58 points — so there's about 4.2 points unexplained. That would mean beans also rose roughly 40% on a 10.5-point base.

So this supports input price inflation across the board, not mix shift and not primarily waste. Both buckets moved.

The one thing I'd want to confirm: have menu prices changed at all over the two years? If input costs rose ~38% and the client held prices flat, that's the real root cause — a failure to pass through. Do we have menu price history?

