[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 0:35] Beans and other inputs both moved, in your build. Menu prices have not changed in two years. Putting that against your read of input inflation, what does it say about the root cause, and what would you do about it?

[candidate · 0:41] That closes it. Root cause: input costs inflated roughly 38% across beans, dairy, packaging and food, and Brew & Bean absorbed all of it because menu prices haven't moved in two years. Revenue grew 15%, which given flat prices must be volume or new stores — so they sold more at progressively worse unit economics.

On the fix: the direct lever is price. Rough math — COGS is 58 points today. If I raise prices by X and volume holds, revenue goes to 100(1+X) and COGS stays at 58 in absolute terms. To get COGS back to 42% of revenue I'd need 58/(1+X) = 42, so 1+X = 1.38, about a 38% increase. That's steep and unrealistic in one move, so I'd phase it and pair it with supply-side action: renegotiate or lock in supplier contracts, hedge coffee, and attack waste and portioning.

Risk is volume loss, so I'd test elasticity in a subset of stores first.

