[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 0:44] A price lever, phased, with supply-side work alongside. Beyond price increases and supplier renegotiation, what else could Brew & Bean do to protect its margin? Give me as many distinct ideas as you can.

[candidate silent 9s]

[candidate · 0:53] Let me group these.

On cost of goods: reduce portion sizes or standardize them so stores aren't over-pouring; cut waste and spoilage through better demand forecasting; simplify the menu so fewer perishable SKUs; substitute inputs — cheaper packaging, alternative milk sourcing; buy in larger volumes or join a purchasing cooperative; vertical integration on roasting if they don't already roast.

On mix rather than price level: push customers toward higher-margin items — brewed coffee and espresso drinks over food and heavy-dairy drinks; add high-margin add-ons like syrups and extra shots; introduce a premium tier that absorbs cost increases on the customers least price-sensitive.

On revenue per transaction without a list-price change: upsell at the register, bundles, loyalty programs that raise visit frequency, shrink cup sizes at the same price.

On other cost lines: labor scheduling against traffic patterns, renegotiate leases, close or convert underperforming stores.

What would you like me to look at next?
